;
GETTING THE SYSTEM RUNNING

A - So you've decided to run an RCP/M
B - Making the first call
C - Host configuration

:A
SO YOU'VE DECIDED TO RUN AN RCP/M

Forty years ago, this would have meant setting aside a computer, buying a
modem, arranging for a telephone line and spending a good many evenings
persuading the various pieces of software to cooperate. Fortunately, most of
that work has already been done for you.

HUMONGOUS RCP/M is a working CP/M computer, not simply a bulletin board with a
CP/M theme. Callers can explore its disks, read documentation, exchange
software, play approved games and visit the board. What sort of collection and
community you build is up to you.

This guide assumes you know your way around CP/M and a host command line. If
you would like a refresher on the caller's side of the system, see the
companion User's Guide. It deals with the peculiarities of running this
particular RCP/M. For exact disk geometry and host-side image tools, keep the
Disk Images reference handy. The Installation notes cover platform-specific
startup matters.

Under the bonnet is a customized version of Udo Munk's cpmsim, from the
z80pack project, emulating a Z80 computer running banked CP/M 3. Our version
adds facilities specifically for RCP/M, including the caller connection and
shared sysop console, swappable library volumes, and the host-side services
used to receive uploads and saved messages. These facilities work in
conjunction with the adapted guest software. Use the cpmsim executable
supplied with HUMONGOUS RCP/M rather than substituting a stock z80pack binary;
the two are not interchangeable for this system. Z3PLUS supplies the command
environment and named directories. BYE510 handles remote operation, and RBBS
provides forums and private mail.

We've also written several utilities specifically for HUMONGOUS RCP/M. AUTH
gives the sysop control over caller permissions and program approval. MOUNT
lets callers exchange software library volumes without restarting CP/M, and
NDSCAN updates the named directories to reflect the contents of a mounted
disk. These three utilities are original contributions of this project, rather
than adaptations of historical CP/M programs.

The normal emulated CPU speed is 4 MHz; changing it affects program timing,
not just the apparent speed of the connection.

You and the caller share this one machine. There is one caller at a time,
alongside your local console. Bear that in mind whenever you sit down to do
some maintenance.

:B
MAKING THE FIRST CALL

Obtain the complete package for your host from the release page. Unpack it
into a writable directory. On macOS run Start-RCPM.command, on Linux
Start-RCPM.sh, and on Windows Start-RCPM.cmd. There is no setup wizard and no
initial configuration is required. Docker users should follow the Docker
instructions; the CP/M side is the same.

The first startup generates the private file SYSOP.DOC in the installation
directory. This contains your initial board credentials. It is not the Sysop's
Guide and should never be made available to callers.

When the modem is ready, call your own system using a terminal such as
SyncTERM. Select raw TCP, address 127.0.0.1, port 4000. Press RETURN. Try:

    HELP
    DIR SIGM:
    DIR CPMUG:
    MOUNT /L
    RBBS

Register an ordinary account, enter a message, read it back and leave the
board with C. At CP/M, type BYE. Call again. You have now seen the machine
from the other end of the connection, which is a useful habit for a sysop to
cultivate.

:C
HOST CONFIGURATION

The external RCPM.CONF is deliberately small and commented. Stop the machine
before editing it.

  Setting - Default - Purpose
  MODE - direct - Direct raw TCP; ip232 selects the bundled tcpser virtual
  modem
  ADDRESS - 127.0.0.1 - Listener IPv4 address
  PORT - 4000 - Caller port in either mode

The launcher handles tcpser's internal endpoints automatically. Keep the
distributed foreground terminal open while serving calls. If you use your own
tmux wrapper, detaching is not shutting down. Docker uses its published port
and persistent data volume; consult its guide before changing the container.

Running a second board is quite possible. Give it its own directory or Docker
volume and a different caller port. Never share writable disk images between
two running machines.
